Send only H.264 reference pictures in the stateless DPB.

Chromium marked every picture in the decoded-picture buffer VALID, and
ACTIVE only when the picture was a reference. The kernel counts ACTIVE
entries. Apple's decoder walks every VALID entry, sizes that table from
the ACTIVE count, and the slice then names a slot by the client's index.
A non-reference kept for display can sit in front of a real reference, so
the index falls off the end of the table and the firmware never finishes
the frame. The VA-API shim packs references only, each one VALID and
ACTIVE. The slice lists already index that same array, so the slot numbers
stay consistent.

This is the stateless H.264 uAPI, not an Apple-only change. The hang shows
up on Apple because that driver sizes the firmware table from the ACTIVE
count while still walking every VALID entry. A frame-based device is
unchanged: it does not use these slot numbers.

--- a/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_video_decoder_delegate_h264.cc	2026-11-18 14:13:37.752323308 -0300
+++ b/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_video_decoder_delegate_h264.cc	2026-11-18 14:13:59.409850200 -0300
@@ -385,6 +385,16 @@
   priv_->dpb_pics.clear();
   size_t i = 0;
   for (const auto& pic : dpb) {
+    // The V4L2 DPB is the reference list the slice indexes into. A picture
+    // kept only so it can be displayed is not a reference. The kernel counts
+    // ACTIVE entries, and Apple's decoder walks every VALID entry but sizes
+    // that table from the ACTIVE count. A non-reference in front of a real
+    // reference makes the slice name a slot past the end of the table, and
+    // the firmware never finishes the frame. The VA-API shim packs the same
+    // list: references only, each one VALID and ACTIVE.
+    if (!pic->ref) {
+      continue;
+    }
     if (i >= std::size(priv_->v4l2_decode_param.dpb)) {
       VLOGF(1) << "Invalid DPB size";
       break;
@@ -417,7 +427,7 @@
     entry.top_field_order_cnt = pic->top_field_order_cnt;
     entry.bottom_field_order_cnt = pic->bottom_field_order_cnt;
     entry.flags = V4L2_H264_DPB_ENTRY_FLAG_VALID |
-                  (pic->ref ? V4L2_H264_DPB_ENTRY_FLAG_ACTIVE : 0) |
+                  V4L2_H264_DPB_ENTRY_FLAG_ACTIVE |
                   (pic->long_term ? V4L2_H264_DPB_ENTRY_FLAG_LONG_TERM : 0);
   }
 
